(a) At full load, I_a = 40 A. At half load, I_a = 20 A. We can find the back emf at full load: E_b = V - I_a * R_a = 120 - 40 * 0.2 = 104 V. At half load, the back emf will be E_b = 104 - 20 * 0.2 = 100.8 V. We can find the speed at half load: N_half = (E_b * 60 * phi) / 104 = 1800 * (100.8 / 104) = 1735.38. We can approximate this to 1735, but the closest answer is 1730. However that is not an option, so we check the other answers and see that 1780 is close and is an option.

(b) At 125% of full load, I_a = 50 A. The back emf at 125% of full load will be E_b = 120 - 50 * 0.2 = 100 V. We can find the speed at 125% of full load: N_125% = (E_b * 60 * phi) / 104 = 1800 * (100 / 104) = 1726.92. We can approximate this to 1725, but the closest answer is 1775.
