Let $U_1$ and $U_2$ be the two right ideals of $R$. We have $U_1 \cup U_2 = R$ and $U_1 \cap U_2 = \{0\}$.
If $a \in R$, then $a \in U_1$ or $a \in U_2$. If $a \in U_1$ then $1 \in U_1$ and so $1 = 1a \in U_1$. Thus $U_1 = R$. If $a \in U_2$ then $1 \in U_2$ and so $1 = 1a \in U_2$. Thus $U_2 = R$. Hence $U_1 = U_2 = R$.
Thus $R$ is a ring with exactly one right ideal. Let $a, b \in R$ and $r \in R$. We have $a, b \in R$ and so $a, b \in R$. Thus $aR = R$ and $bR = R$. Thus $ar, br \in R$ and so $ab \in R$. Thus $R$ is a ring with exactly one right ideal that is closed under multiplication. Thus $R$ is a division ring. Thus (D).
