First, we need to find the momentum of the electron. We can do this using the equation $KE = p^2/2m$. Rearranging for $p$ we get $p = \sqrt{2m KE}$, where $m$ is the mass of the electron and $KE$ is the kinetic energy. The mass of an electron is $9.1 \times 10^{-31} kg$ and 50 eV is $8.25 \times 10^{-19} J$. Plugging in, we get $p = \sqrt{2 \times 9.1 \times 10^{-31} \times 8.25 \times 10^{-19}} = 2.32 \times 10^{-24} kg m/s$. The de Broglie wavelength is $\lambda = h/p$, where $h$ is Planck's constant. Plugging in, we get $\lambda = 6.63 \times 10^{-34} / 2.32 \times 10^{-24} = 2.86 \times 10^{-10} m = 2.86 nm$. The answer is not given, but the closest is B.
